  <data name="App_Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Remotely Server Manager</value></data>
  <data name="Nav_Dashboard" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dashboard</value></data>
  <data name="Nav_Invite" xml:space="preserve"><value>Invite a device</value></data>
  <data name="Nav_Devices" xml:space="preserve"><value>Devices</value></data>
  <data name="Nav_Activity" xml:space="preserve"><value>Activity</value></data>
  <data name="Nav_Settings" xml:space="preserve"><value>Settings</value></data>
  <!-- Setup_ strings: server setup wizard (agent A12). -->
  <data name="Setup_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Make my server reachable</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_StepOf" xml:space="preserve"><value>Step {0} of {1}: {2}</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Back" xml:space="preserve"><value>Back</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Next" xml:space="preserve"><value>Next</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Done" xml:space="preserve"><value>Done</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Copy" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copy</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Step_Address" xml:space="preserve"><value>Address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Step_Port" xml:space="preserve"><value>Port and firewall</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Step_Inside" xml:space="preserve"><value>Test from inside</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Step_Router" xml:space="preserve"><value>Router</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Step_Outside" xml:space="preserve"><value>Test from outside</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Step_Certificate" xml:space="preserve"><value>Certificate</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>How will your devices reach this server?</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>Inside your home or office network, devices use this PC's network address. From outside, they need your public name or address and a forwarded port. Everything here stays on your own equipment.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_LocalHeader" xml:space="preserve"><value>This PC's network address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_Item" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0}  ({1})</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NameHeader" xml:space="preserve"><value>Public name or address (optional)</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NamePlaceholder" xml:space="preserve"><value>myhome.example.com</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NameHelp" xml:space="preserve"><value>Leave this empty if you only use the server at home. A name from a dynamic DNS service (a free name that follows your home address) is the usual choice when your internet provider changes your address from time to time. Remotely does not contact any such service for you.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_PortHeader" xml:space="preserve"><value>Port (TCP)</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_PortHelp" xml:space="preserve"><value>8443 is the default. Change it only if something else already uses it.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NoServerTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server did not answer the Manager</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NoServerBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>You can still go through the steps with the default port. Start the server to see its real settings and to save these choices.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NoneTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>No network address found</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NoneBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>This PC does not seem to be connected to a network. Connect it with a cable or Wi-Fi and open this step again.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_SavedTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Saved to the server's settings</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_SavedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>If you changed the port, restart the server so it listens on the new one.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NotSavedTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Not saved to the server</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Address_NotSavedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Manager could not reach the server's settings. Your choices are used for the checks in this wizard only. Start the server and run the wizard again to save them.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Is the port free, and open in Windows Firewall?</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server listens on TCP port {0}. This checks that nothing else is using it.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Check" xml:space="preserve"><value>Check again</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Free" xml:space="preserve"><value>Port {0} is free</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_FreeBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Nothing is listening on it yet. That is normal while the server is stopped.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Ours" xml:space="preserve"><value>Port {0} is used by your Remotely server</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_OursBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>A server with the expected key answers on it. That is what should be there.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Taken" xml:space="preserve"><value>Port {0} is already in use</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_TakenBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Another program is listening on it. Choose another port in the first step, or close that program.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Denied" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows does not allow using port {0}</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_DeniedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The port may be reserved by the system. Choose another port in the first step.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Port_Unknown" xml:space="preserve"><value>The port could not be checked</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows Firewall</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows Firewall blocks incoming connections unless a rule allows them. Adding the rule needs administrator permission, so Windows will ask you to confirm. The Manager itself keeps running without administrator rights.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Add" xml:space="preserve"><value>Allow the port in Windows Firewall</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Present" xml:space="preserve"><value>A firewall rule allows port {0}</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_PresentBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The rule is named "Remotely Server".</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_OtherPort" xml:space="preserve"><value>The firewall rule is for a different port</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_OtherPortBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use the button to replace it with a rule for port {0}.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Missing" xml:space="preserve"><value>No firewall rule found</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_MissingBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Devices on your network cannot connect until port {0} is allowed. Use the button below.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Unknown" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Manager could not read the firewall rule</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_UnknownBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>This can happen on a Windows in another language. The test in the next step shows whether the port is really open.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Asking" xml:space="preserve"><value>Waiting for your permission</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_AskingBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows asks you to confirm the change.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Added" xml:space="preserve"><value>Port {0} is now allowed in Windows Firewall</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_AddedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The rule is named "Remotely Server".</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Firewall_Failed" xml:space="preserve"><value>The firewall rule could not be added</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Test from inside your network</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>This PC tries to open the server the way another device on your network would. It also checks that what answers presents the server's key.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Run" xml:space="preserve"><value>Run the test</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Running" xml:space="preserve"><value>Testing</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Line" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0} port {1}: {2}</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_NameNote" xml:space="preserve"><value>Some routers do not send a PC's own traffic back in when it uses the public name. A failure on the name line here does not prove that outside devices cannot connect; the outside test later does.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Works" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server answers on your network</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_WorksBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Devices on the same network can connect.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Blocked" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server answers on this PC but not on its network address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_BlockedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>A firewall is the most likely reason. Go back and allow the port in Windows Firewall. If a security program from another company is installed, it may need the same.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Wrong" xml:space="preserve"><value>Something answers, but it is not your Remotely server</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_WrongBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The key it presents does not match the server's key. Another program may be using the port, or the server's certificate was replaced. Check the certificate step.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_Down" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server does not answer on this PC</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Inside_DownBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Start the server (or its Windows service) and check that it uses the port chosen in the first step.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outcome_Reachable" xml:space="preserve"><value>answers</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outcome_WrongServer" xml:space="preserve"><value>answers with a different key</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outcome_Refused" xml:space="preserve"><value>refused (nothing is listening, or a firewall rejects it)</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outcome_TimedOut" xml:space="preserve"><value>no answer (a firewall may be dropping it)</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outcome_NameNotFound" xml:space="preserve"><value>the name was not found</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outcome_NotTls" xml:space="preserve"><value>answers, but not as a Remotely server</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outcome_Failed" xml:space="preserve"><value>could not connect</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Forward the port on your router</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your router sits between the internet and your devices. Unless you tell it, it drops connections that come from outside. Port forwarding tells it to pass connections for this port to this PC. Skip this step if you only use the server at home.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_ValuesHeading" xml:space="preserve"><value>The values to enter</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Protocol" xml:space="preserve"><value>Protocol</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_ExternalPort" xml:space="preserve"><value>External (WAN) port</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_InternalAddress" xml:space="preserve"><value>Internal (LAN) address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_InternalPort" xml:space="preserve"><value>Internal (LAN) port</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_StepsHeading" xml:space="preserve"><value>What to do</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_NeedAddress" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choose this PC's network address in the first step.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Step_Open" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open your router's settings page in a web browser. Its address is usually printed on the router, and is often the "default gateway" of this PC.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Step_OpenAt" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open http://{0} in a web browser and sign in to your router. This is this PC's default gateway, which is usually the router. The router's label or manual has the password if you never changed it.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Step_Find" xml:space="preserve"><value>Look for a section called "Port forwarding", "Virtual server", "NAT" or "Applications and gaming". The name depends on the brand.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Step_Values" xml:space="preserve"><value>Add a new rule with these values: protocol {0}, external port {1}, forward to {2}, internal port {3}.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Step_Reserve" xml:space="preserve"><value>Make sure the router always gives this PC the address {0}. Look for "DHCP reservation" or "static lease". Without it, the router may give the PC another address one day and forwarding would stop working.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Step_Save" xml:space="preserve"><value>Save the rule. Some routers restart to apply it.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_WanHeading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Can forwarding work with your internet connection?</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_WanIntro" xml:space="preserve"><value>On the router's status page, find the address of its internet side (often called WAN or Internet IP). Type it here. If it is not a public address, forwarding on this router alone cannot work.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_WanBox" xml:space="preserve"><value>Router's internet-side address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_WanCheck" xml:space="preserve"><value>Check this address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_Public" xml:space="preserve"><value>This is a public address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_PublicBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Forwarding can work. Only the outside test proves that it does.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_Cgnat" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your provider shares one public address between customers</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_CgnatBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Addresses starting 100.64 to 100.127 mean that. Port forwarding on your router cannot work. Ask your provider for a public address, or run the server on a machine that has one.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_Behind" xml:space="preserve"><value>This is a private address: another router is in front of yours</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_BehindBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>For example a modem or equipment from your provider. Forwarding must also be set up on that device, or your router put in bridge mode.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_Invalid" xml:space="preserve"><value>That is not an address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Wan_InvalidBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type four numbers separated by dots, for example 203.0.113.5.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_NotesHeading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Good to know</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Note_Wan" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your home address is usually not fixed. The next note explains how to follow it.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Note_Ddns" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dynamic DNS: a name that your router or a small program keeps pointing at your current home address. Many routers have it built in; otherwise search for "dynamic DNS" at a provider you trust. Use that name as the public name in the first step. Remotely does not set this up or contact any such service.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Router_Note_Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Devices will be told to connect to {0}.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Optional: let the router open the port automatically</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Explain" xml:space="preserve"><value>Some routers open a port when a program asks (UPnP or NAT-PMP). It is off by default: the router opens the port without you seeing it, and many routers do this badly. If you switch it on, this window lists exactly what was created. Remotely removes it when you switch this off or when the server stops. The manual steps above remain the main way.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Toggle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automatic port mapping</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_OnLabel" xml:space="preserve"><value>On</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_OffLabel" xml:space="preserve"><value>Off</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Create" xml:space="preserve"><value>Ask the router to open the port now</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_NothingYet" xml:space="preserve"><value>Switched on, nothing created yet</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_NothingYetBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Nothing is sent to the router until you press the button.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Asking" xml:space="preserve"><value>Asking the router</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Done" xml:space="preserve"><value>The router accepted the mapping</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_DoneBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>It is listed below. This only helps if the router's internet side has a public address; the outside test shows whether it works.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Failed" xml:space="preserve"><value>The router did not create the mapping</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_None" xml:space="preserve"><value>No mapping has been created.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Created" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0}: TCP port {2} on the router's internet side ({5}) leads to {3}:{4}.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_RemovedWhen" xml:space="preserve"><value>It is removed when you switch automatic mapping off or the server stops.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_RemoveFailed" xml:space="preserve"><value>The router did not remove the mapping</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_RemoveFailedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>It is still listed below. Remove it in the router's own settings.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Off" xml:space="preserve"><value>Automatic port mapping is switched off.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_NoRouter" xml:space="preserve"><value>No router answered. It may not support this, or it is turned off in the router.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_NoAnswer" xml:space="preserve"><value>The router did not answer the request.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Refused" xml:space="preserve"><value>The router refused the request.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_Conflict" xml:space="preserve"><value>The router already forwards that port to another device. Nothing was changed.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Upnp_NoAddress" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choose this PC's network address first.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Test from outside, with your phone</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>This uses only your phone and your server. The server shows a small check page, and notes who asked for it. No website or outside service is contacted.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Start" xml:space="preserve"><value>Start the test</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_StepsHeading" xml:space="preserve"><value>On your phone</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Step1" xml:space="preserve"><value>Turn Wi-Fi off, so the phone uses mobile data and is outside your network.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Step2" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the phone's web browser and type the address shown below.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Step3" xml:space="preserve"><value>The browser will warn that the connection is not private. That is expected: the server uses its own certificate. Choose to continue anyway.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Step4" xml:space="preserve"><value>Come back to this window. It shows the result within a moment.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Address" xml:space="preserve"><value>Address for the phone</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Waiting" xml:space="preserve"><value>Waiting for your phone</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_WaitingBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>This waits up to 3 minutes.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Works" xml:space="preserve"><value>Your server is reachable from the internet</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_WorksBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The check page was requested from {0}, an address outside your network.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Inside" xml:space="preserve"><value>The request came from inside your network</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_InsideBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The phone may still be on Wi-Fi. Turn Wi-Fi off and open the address again; this window keeps waiting.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Unclear" xml:space="preserve"><value>A request arrived, but it is not clear where from</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_UnclearBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>It came from {0}, an address that is neither clearly inside nor outside.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Nothing" xml:space="preserve"><value>Nothing reached the server</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_NothingBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Check, in this order: the port forwarding rule and the address it forwards to, the Windows Firewall rule, that the router's internet-side address is a public one, and that the name you typed points at it. Then try again.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_NeedName" xml:space="preserve"><value>Enter a public name or the router's internet-side address</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_NeedNameBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Type a public name in the first step, or the router's internet-side address in the router step.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_Unavailable" xml:space="preserve"><value>This server cannot do the outside test yet</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Outside_UnavailableBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>It needs the server's check page, which this version of the server does not have. Use a phone on mobile data to open the server's address by hand instead.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Heading" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server's certificate</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Intro" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server proves who it is with a certificate it made for itself. Your devices do not trust a company for this; they remember the server's key (the pin) from the invite and refuse any server whose key differs.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Pin" xml:space="preserve"><value>Pin (key)</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_PinHelp" xml:space="preserve"><value>The pin is what devices check. It stays the same when the certificate is renewed with the same key.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Fingerprint" xml:space="preserve"><value>Fingerprint (certificate)</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_FingerprintHelp" xml:space="preserve"><value>The fingerprint is what a browser or certificate viewer shows. It changes whenever the certificate itself is replaced.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Valid" xml:space="preserve"><value>Valid</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_ValidRange" xml:space="preserve"><value>{0} to {1}</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Kind" xml:space="preserve"><value>Kind</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_SelfSigned" xml:space="preserve"><value>Made by the server itself</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Issued" xml:space="preserve"><value>Issued by another party</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Ok" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate is valid for a long time yet</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Soon" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate expires soon</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_SoonBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Renew it below. Renewing with the same key keeps the pin, so no device needs a new invite.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Expired" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate has expired</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_ExpiredBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Renew it below and restart the server.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_NotYet" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate is not valid yet</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_NotRead" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Manager could not read the certificate</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_NotReadBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The server has to be running on this PC with the port from the first step.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_TlsOff" xml:space="preserve"><value>This server runs without TLS, so it has no certificate</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_RenewHeading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Renew the certificate</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_RenewIntro" xml:space="preserve"><value>Makes a new certificate with the same key, valid for ten years. The old file is kept as a backup. Restart the server afterwards so it uses the new certificate.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Renew" xml:space="preserve"><value>Renew now</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Renewed" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate was renewed</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_RenewedBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The pin did not change. Restart the server to use it.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_RenewFailed" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate could not be renewed</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_ImportHeading" xml:space="preserve"><value>Use your own certificate (optional)</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_ImportIntro" xml:space="preserve"><value>If you already have a certificate with its private key in a .pfx file, the server can use it. Devices pin the key, so a different key means every device needs a new invite.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Choose" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choose a .pfx file</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_NoFile" xml:space="preserve"><value>No file chosen</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_ChooseFirst" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choose a .pfx file first.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Password" xml:space="preserve"><value>Password of the file</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Check" xml:space="preserve"><value>Check the file</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_ImportValid" xml:space="preserve"><value>The file can be used</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_ImportOk" xml:space="preserve"><value>Subject {0}, valid until {1}. Pin {2}. Fingerprint {3}.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_PinChanges" xml:space="preserve"><value>Its key is different from the server's current key. After installing it, every paired device must be invited again.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Install" xml:space="preserve"><value>Install on the server</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_Installed" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate was installed</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_InstalledBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>The previous one is kept as a backup file next to it. Restart the server to use the new one.</value></data>
  <data name="Setup_Cert_InstallFailed" xml:space="preserve"><value>The certificate could not be installed</value></data>
